00:00:00.399 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
thank you for joining us kabul's  international airport is up and running  a civilian commercial flight has taken  off from the afghan capital for the  first time since it fell to taliban  forces last month  these are exclusive pictures of the  plane leaving it's a domestic service  bound for the northern city of maasai  sharif its departure follows repairs to  the runway and radar with the help of  technical teams from qatar  and turkey  let's bring in charles stratford who's  live for us in kabul charlie an  important day today for kabul airport  even if it's not fully operational yet  that's right a very significant day  in terms of afghanistan's hopes for  being able to have access to vast  amounts of desperately needed  humanitarian aid and being able to  freely move around this country and  potentially around the world as well um  we understand that there has been a  second commercial flight taken off since  that that one to maza sarif earlier  another commercial flight having taken  off for herrat  we have seen an incredible effort put in  by technical staff especially from qatar  and we believe turkey also in recent  days  trying to put in as much  repairing of um various of the runway of  various bits of technology reinstalling  things like radar and so on  bear in mind that according to the un  around half this country's population  that's around 20 million people are in  desperate need of urgent humanitarian  aid  so the opening of this airport although  it is just the beginnings is of huge  importance  and earlier today we had the qatar  ambassador who was down on the tarmac  and he was speaking to the media let's  have a listen to what he had to say  via  and we are doing an assessment of the  progress so far also assessing the  damage that was done by the masses of  people who had come to the airport  hopefully in a short period of time we  will be able to achieve everything we  have been able technically to put the  airport in operation and you can see  this aircraft just landed and more will  come tomorrow and the day after tomorrow  depth more diplomatic moves have also  happened today in kabul the head of  pakistan's spy agency the isi faiz hamid  has come into kabul we believe for talks  certainly  we understand possibly with the with the  countries and now pakistan have huge  importance obviously uh  a large neighbor sharing a border of  around 2500 kilometers we understand the  focus of the attention will be on  pakistan's security and refugee concerns  pakistan already plays host to around  three million afghan refugees from  previous conflicts and war  and although the west has accused  pakistan or accused elements within the  pakistani military  and intelligence in the past of actually  helping to fund the taliban which of  course the pakistanis have hugely denied  there is no denying that pakistan does  have influence here over the taliban and  is very keen obviously to see stability  here because of the potential  ramifications across the border pakistan  is also potentially being seen as a  linchpin between the west and the  taliban so yeah diplomatic movements on  that front as well um but uh some  positive news today out of kabul from  the airport thank you for that charlie  charles stratford live for us there in  kabul well let's now speak to aviation  specialist alex macheres who joins us  via skype here in doha alex very good to  have you on the show so as charlie said  there an important day talk us through  the kind of work that went into getting  kabul airport up and running again the  airport i understand was in a terrible  state a lot of damage to the runway  basic infrastructure destroyed  what are some of the equipment and  technical systems that were needed to  get to get it up and running again  well it's still very early days folly  and that's because afghanistan as a  country right now is still without air  traffic control provisions the flights  mentioned by your reporter on the ground  are currently being told that they can  only be domestic flights and so they are  afghan airlines and also they are only  able to operate in the daylight hours  and that is because right now this is  still an airport that is pretty much  totally off limits not just to the rest  of the world but it's still not yet fit  for any kind of significant  return of commercial service that  afghanistan had just a month ago but in  terms of the technical teams that are on  the ground now we know that qatar  in collaboration with turkey are leading  the efforts to ensure the basics the  fact that the runway and the surface of  the runway is secure and intact because  there was a lot of damage that was  reported after the dramatic events on  the 15th and 16th of august in addition  to that we know that they are working to  try to secure an element of air traffic  control provisions  that are primarily for aircraft that  require little to no assistance in  arriving really to deliver aid they are  very reliant on that aid as you  explained and and so having these  military aircraft to be able to come in  is that priority first i was going to  ask you about the priorities now as you  say the airport is not fully operational  what is the top priority right now  because everyone of course wants to get  aid into afghanistan what needs to  happen at the airport for that to be  possible  well there needs to be some kind of  establishment of a security that's first  and we know that qatar say that they are  in talks with the taliban to try to  convince them that they must accept a  foreign security presence if this  airport is going to welcome back any  kind of commercial airline jet be it  passenger or cargo um  muhammad abdul rahman the foreign  minister of qatar told media yesterday  that he was convincing the taliban  aviation security is a little more than  just securing the perimeter of the  airport site itself we need to have  the international community assured that  there are people qualified individuals  on the ground  able to supply air traffic control  provisions able to guarantee the safety  of these aircraft and i think we are  still a way off of that at the moment  right now we will have only these  aircraft that are able to  ultimately use the environment in an  uncontrolled manner but it's not  sustainable for the long term and that's  why these efforts are underway to  ultimately establish something that is  more sustainable and has the technical  support and how long alex do you think  until we see international flights  operate again from that airport  there might be a political element  involved in that i imagine  for sure so what we know at the moment  is that if countries like qatar and  turkey with their nato allies are able  to secure the airport site in a manner  that gives the assurance to the rest of  the world in terms of security and  safety for airliners then that message  will be spread among the aviation  community but to be very honest folly  it's going to take a lot of convincing  and reassurance to have a foreign  airline fly not just over afghanistan  because overflight is off limits now too  but to afghanistan we're talking about  aircraft that are insured by third-party  companies and leasing companies that  will say no airline you are not able to  take this aircraft there because we  can't guarantee that it will get out in  the state that we require it and so on  there are so many complications and  hurdles to overcome the provisions of  fuel for example will these aircraft be  able to be refueled on the ground or not  engineers on the ground you know the  worry about the brain drain and the fact  that a lot of skilled personnel have  left afghanistan what does that mean for  situations unfolding with aircraft that  are going tech for example so all of  these hurdles have to be cleared and  it's going to take a lot not just to get  aircraft to fly over afghanistan a very  key overflight region but to have them  operate to and from the country thank  you so much for talking to us alex thank  you for your insights always good to  have you on the show alex machias  joining us there from doha

